Narrow this slice to approved ordinary usage/statistics publication, not a transcript/history or run-status protocol rewrite. Move those facts out of state-bearing submit results and presentation-triggered computations into their existing canonical UnlockedData and frontend projection. Independent future counters require review of their actual backend owner/type before adding a domain; visibility alone is not approval. Inspect runtime_active_run_internal.*, runtime_submit_internal.*, event_state.*, app adapters, interactive_tui_support.cpp and SessionStats. Pure rendering/interaction state stays in tui-specific files on the tui-thread without locks/UnlockedData, and drafts stay frontend-only until submitted as a string.

Explicitly preserve existing session-history, transcript/tool streaming and local-command content transport, reducers and delivery semantics this iteration. History is too large to snapshot; future backend list/vector access requires separate design. Do not add a bounded journal, cursors, canonical history accumulation or replacement stream protocol. Preserve ordered tool results, request/session authorization, stale-event rejection, compaction boundaries, selection/search anchors, attachment safety and conversation/local-command isolation. Enumerate the retained content paths; this exception cannot retain unrelated run/model/agent/directory/usage facts in old snapshots.

Publish SessionStats at backend lifecycle/mutation points with coherent active_context_tokens/system_prompt_tokens and used-token updates, rejecting stale computations. Remove refresh_session_statistics requests caused by UI snapshot application, rendering or generic completion. In particular /exit must not reload history and republish unchanged statistics twice because completion applies a snapshot and renders; retain legitimate final usage/persistence. Test run transitions, used tokens, coalesced notifications, stale completions, cancel/quit and final statistics. Use existing streaming/queued-follow-up/local-command tests and terminal smokes to prove preserved transport still works; instrument recomputation counts to verify duplicate exit publication is gone.

Centralize normal-operation computation/publication first, at actual relevant backend mutations, rather than adding special cases to every UI action. /exit optimization is a secondary consequence; do not choose the API around shutdown. Preserve existing request/queue/permission/question/plugin/subagent/branch/service interfaces and their operation-status state as well as content. Remove only the ordinary state_snapshot/context-count and UI computation coupling identified in /05/01/05/Result.md; no new generic RunState or pending-operation domain is justified merely because protocol status is visible. Keep costly statistics calculation outside locks and preserve identity/append-target/prompt validation before publication.

Preserve the approved statistics value shape, equality operators and whole-value frontend stats_snapshot_: session::Stats/EntryCounts and the adjacent session::Stats_ts alias now live in session/Stats.h, using ava::frontend::UnlockedData. This later user relocation supersedes Proposal.md's runtime/SessionStats.h placement. Active-history and system-prompt token estimates remain separate canonical members published coherently in this one object. Backend computes estimates/usage; frontend derives the saturated total, compact string and percentage without parsing formatted text back into numbers. Preserve disengaged optional estimates versus known zero.

ModelSelection still copies whole ModelInfo and optional ReasoningSelection, not scoped_model_cycle. SessionStats is the existing sole consumer of the model projection's numeric context-window changed flag; preserve independent token/percentage display flags and recomputation when exact inputs change even if rounded text does not. No speculative typed getters or subscriber framework. Reuse current typed registration/initialization, stable registered destinations, fresh unregistered detached copies and guard-safe access. Notify only dirty state after write-lock release, without copying or terminal work in callbacks; do not promise atomic observation across separate canonical domains or log sensitive values through generic wrapper diagnostics.
